<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for CriKey.app.

  This dictionary is EMPTY, and that is the finding, not an omission. The
  hardened runtime is enabled (`codesign` with the `runtime` option, see build.sh);
  entitlements are the holes you punch in it, and the audit below could not
  justify a single one against the code that exists in this tree today.

  Every entry is a claim about behaviour. Adding one "because a launcher
  usually needs it" would put a privilege in the signature that no call site
  redeems, which is exactly the dishonesty README invariant 7 forbids.

  ==================================================================
  AUDIT: every entitlement considered, and the call site that would
  redeem it
  ==================================================================

  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events              NOT DECLARED
      Would be required to send Apple Events to another application
      (NSAppleScript, AESendMessage, NSAppleEventDescriptor). The macOS
      backend, crates/crikey-platform-macos/src/lib.rs, contains no such
      call: its entire launch path is `Command::new("/usr/bin/open")` in
      `OpenLauncher`, which is a posix_spawn of a system binary and not an
      Apple Event. `MacOsBackend::capability` correspondingly reports
      `Capability::WindowActivation` and `Capability::WindowEnumeration` as
      `Unavailable`. Window activation is the feature that would eventually
      need this; when an implementation lands it will arrive with the
      entitlement, an NSAppleEventsUsageDescription in Info.plist, and the
      capability flipped to `Available` in the same change.

  Accessibility (AXUIElement / AXIsProcessTrustedWithOptions)  N/A
      Accessibility is not an entitlement at all on macOS; it is TCC
      consent granted per-application in System Settings, keyed on the code
      signature, with no plist key to declare. Listed here because it is the
      other half of what a hotkey launcher is usually assumed to need. The
      backend calls no AX API; `Capability::WindowActivation` is
      `Unavailable`.

  Global hotkeys (RegisterEventHotKey / CGEventTap)         N/A
      Also not an entitlement. `RegisterEventHotKey` needs nothing;
      `CGEventTap` needs Input Monitoring TCC consent. Neither is called:
      `Capability::GlobalHotkeys` is `Unavailable` on this backend, which is
      also why Info.plist keeps LSUIElement false.

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation         NOT DECLARED
      Would be required to load code signed by a different team into this
      process. Nothing does. Third-party plugins are not dlopened; they are
      supervised subprocesses over the native, Python and legacy-Python
      transports, so their code is never mapped into CriKey's address space.
      A bundled CPython (staged by `stage-python-runtime.sh`, optional) is
      re-signed with the same identity as the rest of the bundle by
      `build.sh`, so its extension modules satisfy library validation without
      a hole. If a future in-process host ever appears, that host, and not this file,
      is what changes.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit                          NOT DECLARED
  com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory   NOT DECLARED
      No interpreter, tracing JIT or codegen runs inside CriKey's process.
      The renderer is wgpu on Metal, whose shader compilation happens in the
      GPU driver, and every Python interpreter is out of process.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables   NOT DECLARED
      Nothing injects DYLD_* into this process, and accepting them would let
      anything on the machine load a dylib into a signed launcher.

  com.apple.security.cs.debugger                           NOT DECLARED
      CriKey attaches to nothing. Its plugin supervisor manages children with
      pipes and signals, not ptrace or task_for_pid.

  keychain-access-groups                                   NOT DECLARED
      `Capability::SecretStorage` is `Unavailable`; there is no macOS
      `SecretStore` implementation to share a keychain group with.

  com.apple.security.device.audio-input / camera / etc.    NOT DECLARED
      No such hardware is touched.

  App Sandbox (com.apple.security.app-sandbox)             NOT DECLARED
      Deliberately not adopted. CriKey launches arbitrary user-chosen
      applications and supervises arbitrary user-installed plugin
      executables; both are categorically outside a sandbox, and the
      temporary-exception entitlements that would fake it are not accepted
      for Developer ID distribution anyway. This bundle is notarized
      Developer ID, not Mac App Store.

  ==================================================================
  KNOWN, UNFIXED CONSTRAINT
  ==================================================================

  A native plugin the package manager downloaded carries the com.apple.quarantine
  attribute and is signed by whoever built it, or not signed at all. Gatekeeper
  evaluates it on first execution as a separate process; CriKey cannot and does
  not suppress that, and no entitlement here would change it. The plugin
  supervisor surfaces the spawn failure rather than pretending the plugin
  loaded.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
